 correspondent based in istanbul  this is what makes the job  so fantastic  so  [Music]  did you know if we tried to use less  energy we could help reduce air  pollution  what like turning off the lights and  there'll  stuff  a mild windy start with rain from  cornwall up into southern scotland and  heavy showers to the west northern  england will stay wet through the  morning and wales in southwest england  will turn wet but southern scotland will  largely dry up elsewhere it'll be mostly  fine but the north-western scotland can  expect heavy showers it'll stay very  mild scotland will see showers clearing  later there'll be little change  elsewhere the unsettled conditions mean  air pollution levels should remain low  with levels between 1 and 3 on the daily  air quality index  the air quality report sponsored by  philips air purifiers  and welcome back to the daily climate  show on sky news and we're getting  straight to discussing the climate  issues of the day with greenpeace uk's  head of climate kate blagojevich and  organic farmer and deputy president of  the national farmers union stuart  roberts a very warm welcome to you both  thank you for joining us  well perhaps surprisingly considering  the global climate change summit  starting in glasgow in just a few days  time today's budget was not dominated by  spending on climate the government did  of course announce a whole raft of new  climate measures including its net zero  strategy just last week so was it  inevitable given the post-covert  environment that green measures would be  overshadowed in today's budget kate to  you first your reaction to that  i think that the climate emergency  should have been at the heart of this  autumn budget uh obviously there were a  raft of policies announced last week but  without the money to back up those  policies they're just words on paper and  that doesn't lead to any action at all  and that means that the climate targets  that those policies  are supposed to be hitting just simply  aren't going to be reached without the  money to back them up it felt very much  like richie sunak was much more  interested in investing in prosecco than  he was the planet and stuart your  thoughts after today's budget of course  a lot of criticism at the lack of green  spending of course just days ahead of  cop  as the host nation what did you make of  that was that odd to you the optics of  that perhaps  i think one of the things for me is an  and kate touched on this you you can't  look at the budget in isolation i think  you've got to look at it alongside  the the net zero strategy  and look there are still some big  funding gaps there that need to be  filled as kate said but i also look at  it through the lens of of my sector my  industry there were some things in the  budget so for example the announcement  about business rate investment relief  for on-site renewables or green  technologies that will definitely help  our sector get towards net zero there is  still a lot more to be done  but it wasn't all disappointment but  there are still those funding gaps  missing  and a strategy without funding isn't uh  isn't a strategy we've got to have the  funding that goes with it as well  yeah kate see you next uh as stuart  referenced there you know the government  has allocated 30 billion pounds to  create new green industries of the  future since march as the chancellor  referenced once again today do you think  that budget day is simply far too  complex  a day far too complex in terms of its  level of announcements in public  spending to make those sorts of big  green announcements  i think no because i do think that the  green announcement should have been very  clear from the outset the moment that he  stood up he should have been talking  about the climate crisis we're days away  from cop we can see that extreme weather  events are happening today they're  happening every single day all around  the world we're hearing from un  scientists  with new reports every single day saying  how  we the uk but also globally are not  hitting the climate targets that we  should be in order to try and keep  temperature rises to 1.5 the chancellor  and this government do need to be doing  much more than making announcements and  saying targets they need to be  allocating the money to back them up  and stuart just lastly on this issue uh  what do you make of the decision to cut  air passenger duty by 50 on domestic  flights of course fuel duties are almost  at a record high at the moment uh  low-income families are really  struggling  was this a surprise to you  i'm trying to say it was a surprise as  someone who actually took a decision a  few years ago not to take domestic  flights precisely for uh the  environmental reasons um i was very  surprised by that one and uh yeah that  came a bit left field i thought  okay  on to our next issue now and of course  it's a rare day when we don't mention a  climate report on this show from a big  international institution more often  than not they're telling policy makers  that targets simply aren't enough that  not enough is being spent and they were  heading for climate catastrophe so is  the message getting through to people  how difficult is it for people to work  out how they can personally help on  climate  kate  to you first you've been with greenpeace  since 2014 have you sensed a change in  the public mood  i have yes i think that over the last  few years we've seen that public the  public are much more interested in  climate change they're much more  concerned about climate change and  that's true i think even now  uh while we're in the middle of a  pandemic where some people might said  actually climate change is going to go  on a back burner for a lot of people  actually what's happening is that the  climate emergency and tackling it is  still one of the top voting issues for  people we've seen loads of polling  coming out this year basically all  saying that the majority of people want  the government to be taking much more  climate action they want to be spending  a lot more money to make sure that the  crisis is taken seriously and that the  government actually acts like we're in a  climate crisis which we are as well as  obviously  dealing with the pandemic and  understanding that the two can actually  go hand in hand and that we should be  really making sure that we're we've got  a green recovery coming out of the  covert pandemic  that's what people are really interested  in now  yeah very interesting and stewart your  thoughts on that typically people  wouldn't care about global issues in the  middle of a recession are you sensing a  change too in terms of what people care  about and what you're hearing within the  farming industry and agriculture  i i absolutely am i think people see  this as a as an opportunity to to take  stock to change things i think as kate  says this green recovery point if i look  at the farming industry itself over the  last three four five years as we've been  starting to talk about net zero as an  industry farmers are really feeling  empowered they feel as though they can  be part of that solution and that  empowerment i think is really important  but it's also important i think at every  life stage so one of the most exciting  things we've been doing is actually  doing some stem projects in classrooms  with kids  and actually getting them to see where  agriculture  butts up against and can be a solution  to climate change getting youngsters  involved getting everyone involved and  ultimately for me it's about empowering  every single one of us as an individual  to be part of that solution  and actually it's just part of our  everyday life doing something every day  to do our bit for the planet  and kate there's far more information  and dialogue now around about climate  change but many people have quite a high  proportion of people find it quite  confusing what they're reading on social  media of course mp's debating today  educating students on climate issues but  do you think they should be focusing on  other generations as well  i think honestly i do think that climate  information needs to be very clear very  accessible and it's what i spend quite a  lot of my time thinking about um so that  we can inspire everybody to get involved  and to do their bit whether that's  signing petitions whether that's getting  involved in local air pollution  campaigns whether that's writing to  their mps obviously we've seen it the  future generation of school strikers  have been hugely inspiring in terms of  what they've been doing and the protests  that they've been holding  so i think that there is a huge amount  of work to be done still to make sure  that the information is getting out  there so that people can really  understand it and know what needs to be  done  really appreciate your thoughts today  kate blagojevich and stuart thank you  very much indeed  well that's everything from us for today  joining me on tomorrow's show is climate  scientist ella gilbert and international  ambassador of the conservative  environment network stanley johnson but  do stay tuned because coming up next of  course more on the budget the chancellor  promising billions but has he delivered  i'll see you tomorrow  [Music]
 

